1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,630
Heel pain usually gets blamed on being on your feet all day - but the setup for it actually starts while you sleep.

2
00:00:06,950 --> 00:00:15,290
Your plantar fascia, the tissue supporting your arch, sits in a shortened position all night, and three habits pile more strain on top of that.

3
00:00:15,610 --> 00:00:21,949
Ramping up your mileage too fast doesn't give the fascia time to adapt, so overload leads to repeated microtears.

4
00:00:22,269 --> 00:00:29,990
Standing for hours in flat, cushionless shoes on hard floors means every step's impact lands straight on that tissue instead of being absorbed.

5
00:00:30,310 --> 00:00:36,716
And taking that very first step out of bed without stretching first yanks the overnight-shortened fascia straight open, all at once.

6
00:00:37,036 --> 00:00:39,483
Build up activity gradually, week by week.

7
00:00:39,803 --> 00:00:42,842
Pick shoes with real arch support for long standing days.

8
00:00:43,162 --> 00:00:53,080
And before you even swing your legs out of bed, circle your ankles and stretch your calves first - loosen that tissue before it takes your full weight, and the first-step pain eases off fast.
